{
  "claims": {
    "C1": {
      "status": "NOT-TESTED",
      "coverage_pct": 0,
      "justification": "Your replication did not retrain/evaluate the paper’s heterogeneous GNN on SUNSET-1 nor compare against the stated tabular/image/homogeneous-GNN baselines with the reported ID/OOD MSE/NMSE metrics. Therefore the representation/accuracy claim cannot be assessed from the provided evidence."
    },
    "C2": {
      "status": "NOT-TESTED",
      "coverage_pct": 0,
      "justification": "The on-the-fly random-subdivision augmentation effect on OOD error (22.1 -> 16.5 MSE) is a training-time/data-pipeline claim on SUNSET-1. Your evidence does not include augmentation ablations or SUNSET-1 OOD evaluation, so this claim cannot be assessed."
    },
    "C3": {
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "coverage_pct": 70,
      "justification": "Supported in methodology and qualitative outcome: using a differentiable, physics-motivated surrogate with autograd gradients, your gradient-based approach (trust-region-like local optimizer with restarts akin to basinhopping) consistently outperformed gradient-free baselines under identical call budgets across 2/4/6/8-region parameterizations, and showed large sample-efficiency gains (reported ~10x to ~99x vs random). Also supported: the optimizer found designs substantially brighter than a training-set-best analog (~5.0x improvement). Not fully reproduced: the paper’s specific pipeline (hetero-GNN surrogate trained on SUNSET + SciPy trust-constr + basinhopping) and the headline magnitude (up to 6.5x brighter) were not matched exactly (you observed ~5x on an analog surrogate), and the “~2x within training distribution” sub-claim was not directly quantified in your evidence."
    },
    "C4": {
      "status": "PARTIAL",
      "coverage_pct": 60,
      "justification": "Partially supported: your optimized structures often exhibit Nd enriched toward outer regions and Er enriched toward inner regions, and you report Yb-rich buffering/transport behavior as present (design_rules_check shows yb_buffer_present=true for all n). However, the heuristic recovery is not consistent across all tested layer counts: for n=6, both Nd_outer_gt_inner and Er_inner_gt_outer are false; for n=8, Er_inner_gt_outer is false. Thus the claimed spontaneous recovery of established design rules is observed in some cases (notably n=2 and n=4) but not robustly across all optimized architectures in your surrogate setting."
    },
    "C5": {
      "status": "NOT-REPRODUCED",
      "coverage_pct": 20,
      "justification": "Your experiments fix particle radius at 15 nm for the reported best structures and vary only the number of regions/layers (2/4/6/8). You do not provide an optimization matrix over particle size nor results demonstrating monotonic brightening with increasing radius, nor evidence that 7–10 layer architectures outperform simpler ones specifically for >10 nm radius. While your best result occurs at higher layer count (8 regions), that alone does not test the paper’s size-scaling claim or the stated 7–10 layer advantage regime."
    }
  },
  "overall_verdict": "SPOT-CHECK",
  "overall_justification": "Only the inverse-design/optimization-related claims were meaningfully exercised, and they are supported in direction (gradient-based optimization on a differentiable surrogate is markedly more sample-efficient and finds substantially brighter designs than gradient-free baselines and a training-best analog), but not fully matched in exact pipeline or headline improvement factor. Design-rule recovery is mixed across layer counts. Core supervised-learning claims (C1–C2) and size-scaling/layer-regime claim (C5) were not tested or not supported by the provided evidence."
}
